## Lore

In the Republic of Japan cyborgs are held in the highest esteem as mechanized
super-warriors. Power armor and robots may carry heavier weapons and armor, yet
thousands volunteer to become cyborg soldiers for the military and the law
enforcement agencies. A ''borg never feels trapped in a tin can and is never
caught outside his armor: he is a mega-damage war machine around the clock, and
the bionic body answers at the speed of thought as though it were flesh.

Japanese society attaches no stigma to being a ''borg. Most are regarded as
heroes; children run up to cheer them rather than flee, which does much to ease
the strain of being both less and more than human, and many kids grow up
looking forward to the day they can become a ''borg and serve their people.

Even so, every candidate for full conversion is carefully screened and tested,
and prepared for life as a living machine; fewer than 2% wash out. A fifth of
the Republic''s Armed Forces are full conversion ''borgs, 13% are partial
conversions, and 60% of soldiers carry minor implants or prosthetics. Ichto can
build any Republic cyborg too, including the dragon ''borgs, and its conversion
of mercenaries and adventurers without the full screening is another sore point
between the two.

## GM Notes

**Light and heavy bionics.** The Republic, Ichto and H-Brand use nano-technology
to build very light bionics that give up nothing in strength or speed. Light
systems are general purpose, for infiltration, security and police work, and a
light ''borg can pass for human. Heavy systems are for front-line combat and
heavy support: large, bulky, unmistakably inhuman, able to carry heavy plate and
giant-sized weapons. Special operatives, variants and exceptions can be built
with the ''borg design rules of the Rifts RPG.

**Quarters.** Four soldiers share a dormitory-style barracks unit, each with a
private bedroom and study with a computer, games, stereo and television, and a
shared bathroom, sitting room and living room. A soldier may live off base at
his own expense. Rank is standard military, private to general.

**Decommissioned cyborgs.** A ''borg leaving military or police service has
three options. (1) Keep basic bionics and remain a citizen: external weapons and
spy systems are removed, and the character registers with the Cyborg Civilian
Commission, which helps him adjust, find work and watches his conduct; many
join the reserves or work as police, detectives, bodyguards, bouncers or in
construction. (2) Special consideration for long (25-60 years) or distinguished
service: the character may keep most systems, including light or medium armor
and basic bionic weapons, if authorized to move into private security or a
police agency, or if leaving to become an adventurer or mercenary, in which case
he gives up his citizenship and may only visit the Republic as an approved
foreign visitor. (3) Retro-conversion: bionics are replaced with lifelike
bio-systems grown from the character''s own DNA; a full conversion keeps an
artificial core body and downgraded bionics. P.S., P.P. and Spd become 18 for an
honorable retirement and 10 for criminals or the dishonorably discharged, some
of whom go AWOL rather than face it. The alternative is a clone body, fast-grown
from the character''s DNA in three years or a genetic blank in three months; the
practice is under heavy moral and political fire and may be banned within 20
years. Retro-fitting is also imposed on ''borg criminals and dissidents.

**Life expectancy.** Retro-fitted: 50+2D4 years from the refit, roughly the
Republic''s human average of 140. Clone body: 90+3D6 years for a fast-grown
clone of the character''s own DNA, 50+4D6 years for a genetic blank. Partial
conversion: about that of a normal Republic human, 140 to 180 years. Full
conversion: 300+ years with regular maintenance and no brain damage, trauma or
mental deterioration.

**Laws governing cybernetic systems.** The Republic''s laws are harsh and
strictly upheld. Illegal systems not used in a crime are removed at the owner''s
expense plus 10%, with a permanent record and probation; repeat or heinous
offenders can be barred from augmentation for life, and breaking that bar means
life imprisonment or death. Custom alteration of legal systems is usually a
misdemeanor. Concealed weapons without a permit and unauthorized bionics are
removed (installing bionics abroad is legal; bringing them back is not). Using
illegal systems in a crime adds 3 to 10 years and a 10,000 credit fine. Reckless
use costs 500-1000 credits; assault with an authorized implant 1000-10,000 plus
restitution and possibly jail and removal; premeditated assault means removal of
all bionics and 1D4 years. Premeditated murder with bionics, or killing someone
for their parts, is life imprisonment or execution. Removing someone''s implants
against their will is 20-40 years; running a body chop-shop 20 years; trading in
stolen cybernetics 5 years and stolen military bionics 20 years, possibly
treason. No one under 16 may receive implants except for medical need, and no
one under 20 may undergo bionic reconstruction or conversion.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'republic-cyborg-soldier');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'republic-cyborg-soldier';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-republic-cyborg-soldier-class.sql');
